A||||6||Richard Parnell||Coil-over conversion kit||||5||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||09-15-2000||10:57 PM||||Got a question about the Shelby coil-over conversion kit.  I'm thinking of ordering it for my 87 SC which is now in the process of having a V8 put in using Archies kit.  Are there any problems with using this kit?  Is there a better way to go?  The price is not bad, $214.00.  And I can lower the car if I want.  Will it be beefy enough for the V8?||||reg||5
Z||000001||rockcrawl||09-15-2000||11:26 PM||||Wow, $214 !! I hope you're getting some really good struts with that. Seems kinda expensive. Anyway, I've never heard of the Shelby kit, but you can get the coil-over springs in just about any rate you want. I used 350 lb 12" springs with my V8/4spd auto and it worked pretty well. The springs should cost about $40 ea, plus a couple bucks for the threaded sleeves and nuts. Then all you need is a top plate with the three mounting holes and a hole in the center for the strut, and a hub for the spring. This piece is very easy to make! You can use any struts you choose as long as the body diameter is 2". You need to cut the orig spring perch off the strut and slide the threaded sleeve over it. Here's a decent picture of all that's required. <A HREF="http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/coilk.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/coilk.htm</A>  It sure doesn't look like $214 to me! You can get the springs from Jegs or Summit, but they don't carry the sleeves and nuts anymore. I get them at a speed shop down the street.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||7
Z||000002||mshill||09-15-2000||11:51 PM||||Has anyone made their own.  I was about to cough up the $220 but if it can be done identically for half the price I want to know.  Anyone making the triangular mounting piece?  Anywhere else we can purchase the threaded sleeve and nut?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||09-16-2000||12:59 AM||||Mshill,<P>Down off Baseline is Naake. He does racing suspensions. I got the sleeves and rings for something like $60. I have the Ryane top plates. All I need is the coils. Naake said he would loan me some coils in varying rates until I found what I wanted. 200-300lbs is what I figure is the range.<P>The top plates could be duplicated very easy. Coils run around $50 each.||||reg||1
Z||000004||rockcrawl||09-16-2000||09:03 AM|||| <A HREF="http://www.jegs.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.jegs.com/</A>   <A HREF="http://www.summitracing.com/index.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.summitracing.com/index.htm</A> <P> Both of those places sell HAL springs for $36.99 ea. They used to sell the other parts for $25/ both sides, but not any more. I think I paid $20/ side for the sleeves and nuts at the local shop. If you need details of the top plates to have them made, let me know.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||09-16-2000||10:14 AM||||Rockcrawl,<P>Yeah, a new source for tops plates would help people out once Ryane goes belly up on Fiero parts. I missed the coils in Jegs. Have to look again.||||reg||14
Z||000006||Richard Parnell||09-18-2000||12:29 AM||||Thanks for the answers.  I've been busy the last few. Rockcrawl, the kit i was looking at has all the one you displayed but also has the struts.  So I don'tice.  Also the question I asked had more to do with problems on the bolts matching up and small details about fit if anyone knows of such problems.  Thanks.||||reg||7
